\begin_layout Subsection
Effective Dimension of Base Function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Define
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
H_{\Upsilon}=\int_{\Omega}\left[\sigma(w_{l}x+b_{l})\right]_{l=1\cdots L}\left[\sigma(w_{l}x+b_{l})\right]_{l=1\cdots L}^{T}\rho(x)\,\mathrm{d}x,
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tilde{H}_{\Upsilon}=\sum_{j=1}^{J}\left[\sigma(w_{l}x_{j}+b_{l})\right]_{l=1\cdots L}\left[\sigma(w_{l}x_{j}+b_{l})\right]_{l=1\cdots L}^{T}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is clear that 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{\Upsilon}$
\end_inset

 has the same eigenvalue decaying property as 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{c(\theta)}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{L(\theta)}$
\end_inset

.
 And 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{H}_{\Upsilon}$
\end_inset

....
 Idealy, the parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[a_{l}\right]_{l=1\cdots L}$
\end_inset

 can be solved through
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{bmatrix}\sigma(w_{1}x_{1}+b_{1}) & \sigma(w_{2}x_{1}+b_{2}) & \cdots & \sigma(w_{L}x_{1}+b_{L})\\
\sigma(w_{1}x_{2}+b_{1}) & \sigma(w_{2}x_{2}+b_{2}) & \cdots & \sigma(w_{L}x_{2}+b_{L})\\
\vdots &  &  & \vdots\\
\sigma(w_{1}x_{J}+b_{1}) & \sigma(w_{2}x_{J}+b_{2}) & \cdots & \sigma(w_{L}x_{J}+b_{L})
\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}a_{1}\\
a_{2}\\
\vdots\\
a_{L}
\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}y(x_{1})\\
y(x_{2})\\
\vdots\\
y(x_{J})
\end{bmatrix}
\]

\end_inset

by its normal equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tilde{H}_{\Upsilon}\begin{bmatrix}a_{1}\\
a_{2}\\
\vdots\\
a_{L}
\end{bmatrix}=\sum_{j=1}^{J}\left[\sigma(w_{l}x_{j}+b_{l})\right]_{l=1\cdots L}y(x_{j})
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However, due to the analysis above 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{H}_{\Upsilon}$
\end_inset

 is numerically not full rank.
 To overcome this diffculty, one possibility is to desigin 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{l}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{l}$
\end_inset

 so that 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{H}_{\Upsilon}$
\end_inset

 is reasonable full rank.
 But this could?? break the generarization of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Upsilon_{\theta}(x)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

